  "output": "### **Executive conclusion**\nThe training program saw significant architectural and tooling changes across all trainees. Key shifts include the adoption of Qdrant for vector storage, a move towards modular, service-oriented architectures using FastAPI, and the implementation of caching strategies to optimize performance and cost. There was also a strong emphasis on developing robust prompt engineering, context engineering, and harness engineering practices, moving away from hardcoded prompts and towards dynamic, structured inputs for LLMs. The integration of GitHub MCP for automated code reviews and data ingestion from various sources (e.g., Excel, PDF) into a centralized database (Superbase PostgreSQL) were also important developments.\n\n### **Important Changes**\n- **Qdrant Adoption**: Dakshinya Nachimuthu and Himaya Perumal were instructed to deploy their vector stores to Qdrant Cloud, moving away from local storage. This change aims to centralize embeddings and enable shared access for other agents. [17 August 2026, 13-14 \u2014 Siddharth Saminathan]\n- **Service-Oriented Architecture with FastAPI**: Dakshinya Nachimuthu was directed to refactor her RAG system into three distinct services (vector store ingestion, retrieval, and evaluation) wrapped in FastAPI endpoints for reusability and easier deployment. [17 August 2026, 9-11 \u2014 Siddharth Saminathan]\n- **Modular Reranker Design**: The reranker component was to be made a separate, reusable module within Dakshinya's retrieval service, allowing other components or users to integrate it independently. [17 August 2026, 8-9 \u2014 Siddharth Saminathan]\n- **Prompt and Context Engineering Emphasis**: A strong directive was given to move away from hardcoded prompts, advocating for a \"prompt builder\" service and dedicated Python files for context engineering to manage LLM inputs dynamically and efficiently. [17 August 2026, 9-11 \u2014 Siddharth Saminathan]\n- **Centralized Knowledge Base (Superbase PostgreSQL)**: Ganesh Krishna was tasked with building a structured knowledge base, initially using Superbase PostgreSQL, to store extracted facts, assignments, QA events, and concept coverage, aiming to reduce LLM reliance on raw transcripts for certain queries. [17 August 2026, 1-2 \u2014 Siddharth Saminathan]\n- **GitHub MCP Integration for Data Ingestion**: The team was instructed to connect to GitHub MCP for automatic data ingestion, specifically for codebases and information, to enable agents to read and process code. [17 August 2026, 12-13 \u2014 Siddharth Saminathan]\n- **Caching Implementation**: Himaya Perumal was tasked with implementing embedding caching to reduce re-computation costs and latency, storing embeddings in a local cache or cloud environment. [21 July 2026, 1-2 \u2014 Siddharth Saminathan]\n- **Shift to LangGraph for Orchestration**: Ganesh Krishna was advised to transition his Excel agent's workflow to LangGraph to enable more complex, asynchronous, and parallel task orchestration, moving beyond linear LangChain workflows. [27 July 2026, 13 \u2014 Siddharth Saminathan]\n- **Deepseek Model Usage**: Ganesh Krishna was using Deepseek V4 Flash and V4 Pro models for his Excel editing agent, with a focus on understanding the differences and specific use cases for each. [31 July 2026, 14-16 \u2014 Siddharth Saminathan]\n- **Introduction of Scroll API**: Dakshinya Nachimuthu was tasked with implementing and experimenting with the Scroll API to improve retrieval completeness by iterating through entire documents, especially when similarity search might miss information. [22 July 2026, 1-2 \u2014 Siddharth Saminathan]\n- **HNSW and Payload Index for Vector Search**: The underlying vector search mechanism in Qdrant was explained to include HNSW for efficient searching and payload index for metadata filtering, which are critical for fast retrieval. [4 August 2026, 6-7 \u2014 Siddharth Saminathan]"
